WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.339
Film Courage: Asghar, is it true that you
view screenwriting almost like a math equation?

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.370
You piece parts together?&nbsp;

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:12.480
What is your process for screenwriting?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:37.430
Asghar Farhadi (via translator):&nbsp; The thing
that might interest&nbsp; you and that I’ll

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:42.730
explain to you is that when I start [writing]
I am writing without any kind of calculation,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:51.120
any kind of architectural overview.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:01:00.309
It seems when you actually see the film, that
I’ve begun with the puzzle pieces that I

00:01:00.309 --> 00:01:21.270
keep moving around until I find the final
shape but it’s truly 

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.979
the opposite.

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:31.810
Once I’ve written a first draft summary,
it’s only then that I begin to correct and

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:42.460
to change the puzzle pieces in a conscious
manner.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:50.240
Meaning I write in the first stage with my
heart and the rest of it with my mind.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.690
Film Courage:&nbsp; You have many people waiting
for your movies.&nbsp;

00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:01.979
Do you ever feel a pressure or what are your
thoughts on an audience that can be fickle

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:02.979
at times?&nbsp;

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:06.930
I know you’ve said previously that awards,
although they’re nice and complimentary

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.480
toward your work, are not the only reason
that you make films.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:31.390
Asghar Farhadi (via translator):&nbsp; As I’ve
stated before these events allow the audiences

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:36.880
to become more familiar with the language
of my films and (for a greater number of them)

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:47.980
to see my films.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:54.850
What I don’t like is that they go see the
film to see me more than to see the film and

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.900
where it is strong and where it might not
be strong.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:08.270
Film Courage:&nbsp; How do you feel about your
films being framed in a cultural view instead

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:13.700
of a mere thought of just stories about relationships,
about people?&nbsp;

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.390
So when you make a movie from a Western standpoint,
it’s seen from a cultural view with some

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.390
sort of statement when it’s more about human
beings interacting?

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:46.230
Asghar Farhadi (via translator):&nbsp; It 

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:52.070
really depends on the viewer and what their
preference is and what angle they look at

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.380
the film from.&nbsp;

00:03:53.380 --> 00:04:00.540
It’s possible that some viewers in a far
away city may see the film and no cultural

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.170
aspect is important to them.&nbsp;

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:15.580
They are just interested in seeing these characters
and where they’re going.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:24.190
However, as this is a realistic film and it
is made in a society and the characteristics

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.850
of that society are reflected in and can be
found in the film.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:36.150
Film Courage:&nbsp; I know as a teenager (in the
1980’s) you were involved in a cinema society.&nbsp;

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:44.180
What would you advise to a young, Iranian
filmmaker today who wants to make movies?

00:04:44.180 --> 00:05:09.380
Asghar Farhadi (via translator):&nbsp; I’ve
always refrained from giving specific advice

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:15.780
to try and guide someone because the advice
could in fact lead them the wrong way.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:23.070
But the one thing I can say is to tap into
their unconscious to make their films, rather

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:45.740
than their conscious mind to start with.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:52.670
We all have this incredible wealth that is
our unconscious and over time it some how

00:05:52.670 --> 00:06:04.180
has been veiled and sunk to some depth that
we can’t access it, but if we could access

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:08.110
it there are unexpected and wonderful things
there.

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:13.690
Film Courage:&nbsp; Going back to those early
days of the cinema society, what was your

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.210
goal in terms of making movies?&nbsp;

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:21.890
I think it was an Eastern European film you
saw that really changed things for you, maybe?&nbsp;

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:28.520
But what did you want to say with film and
what was your goal in making them?

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:43.810
Asghar Farhadi (via translator):&nbsp; There’s
a number of things.&nbsp;

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:51.000
The one thing is that I really derive great
pleasure from filmmaking and if I were not

00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:10.889
able to do this, life would be difficult 

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:15.449
for me.

00:07:15.449 --> 00:07:20.010
The other thing that is important and perhaps
has become even more so recently, is for me

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:27.320
to address questions I have in my films and
then in the course of the ensuing discussions

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.650
learn and grow.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:29.970
Film Courage:&nbsp; Excellent.&nbsp;

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:30.970
Thank you.

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.610
Asghar Farhadi:&nbsp; You’re welcome.&nbsp;

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:33.490
Thank you.

